WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.339 --> 00:00:01.280
Hello, everyone.

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:06.900
This game was played in 1958 between Bobby Fischer and Samuel Reshevsky.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:16.700
Although this is a very short game, it's worth noticing that Samuel Reshevsky was a magnificient player.

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:22.829
He had a very long chess career and in this career he played against 11 world champions.

00:00:23.410 --> 00:00:29.900
Of those 11 he defeated seven of them, including champions like Lasker Casablanca and Alexander Alekhine.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:37.800
So it's really hard to imagine that he could fall for a trap like this.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:45.840
But although you can find this game on the internet as "Bobby Fischer's Trap" or "Bobby's Trap",

00:00:46.719 --> 00:00:55.300
after this game Bobby actually said that he just read about this trap in an old Russian magazine from the 50s.

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:05.700
Bobby also said somewhere around 1950s he considered Samuel Reshevsky to be the strongest player in the world.

00:01:06.670 --> 00:01:10.199
So let's see this short game between these two great rivals.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.020
Bobby is white and he plays e4.

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:16.349
Reshevsky is black. He goes c5, the Sicilian Defense.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:18.549
Knight to f3.

00:01:18.549 --> 00:01:19.260
Knight c6.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:21.260
d4.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:25.860
C takes d4. Knight takes d4. And now g6, the Accelerated Dragon.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:30.180
Knight c3. Bobby goes for the Najdorf Variation.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:36.760
Not for the c4 -- the Maróczy Bind, which is more popular nowadays.

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:39.340
Bishop to g7.

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:41.360
Bishop to e3.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:42.600
Knight to f6.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.180
And here Fischer plays Bishop to c4, his signature move.

00:01:48.100 --> 00:01:49.720
Black castles.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:52.580
Black is also setting a trap here.

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:58.560
He's hoping for white to play f3 and organize an attack with queen d2 with bishop h6.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:06.400
But this would be a blunder because the queen to b6 is a very ugly move for white.

00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.660
As he's attacking the B2 pawn.

00:02:10.119 --> 00:02:16.749
After white protects it somehow like bishop to d3, now black has this resource of playing knight takes on e4.

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:21.800
And this is at least an equal position for black.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.940
Which Fisher doesn't allow of course.

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:30.500
So in this position Fisher plays another of his signature moves, bishop to b3.

00:02:31.819 --> 00:02:36.600
This is a tricky move. This is the position that's called Bobby's Trap.

00:02:37.489 --> 00:02:43.059
He's inviting black to play knight to a5 and try to exchange this stronger light-square bishop.

00:02:43.730 --> 00:02:48.900
Which, for no reason, Samuel Reshevsky plays and falls for the trap.

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:50.799
He plays knight to a5.

00:02:52.129 --> 00:02:56.499
And this is what happens next. Bobby plays e5.

00:02:57.500 --> 00:03:04.900
Now if Reshevsky captures on b3 then he simply loses a piece after E takes f6 and knight takes on b1.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:06.500
Okay, we can just play it.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:14.140
Here. And now this is knight is trapped because black has capture the pawn on g7.

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:23.200
So after e5, if the knight goes on h5 then simply g4 and the knight is captured.

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:29.000
So there's only one place for the knight to go and that's knight to e7.

00:03:29.060 --> 00:03:32.000
But this is a very big blunder.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:37.000
As Fischer plays bishop takes on F7.

00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:41.500
In here, black can easily resign.

00:03:42.080 --> 00:03:47.530
Which Reshevsky probably saw, but as I said they were great rivals

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:53.800
and he didn't want to allow Fischer to get this miniature game...

00:03:57.340 --> 00:03:58.720
...accomplished.

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:05.560
Like I said, he could resign here, but he played the rook takes on f7.

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:13.120
Here Fischer plays knight e6 and traps the black queen. There is no place for black queen to go.

00:04:14.329 --> 00:04:21.789
Again, this is another position where Reshevsky could have just resigned, but he decided to capture and give up his queen.

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:28.180
He played a couple more moves and only after a couple of more moves he resigned.

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:32.829
But it's interesting what happens if king captures on f7.

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:37.390
If king captures on f7 then again knight to e6, trapping the queen.

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:40.720
But now the king can capture here. King takes on e6.

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:45.160
But this is even worse as black is getting checkmated.

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:46.689
Queen to d5 check.

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:48.740
King goes to f5.

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:50.120
g4 check.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:51.140
King captures.

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:52.420
Rook to g1 check.

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:57.340
King to H4. Queen to e4 check.

00:04:57.340 --> 00:05:00.260
And after king h5, queen to e4 checkmate.

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:04.150
A couple more moves are possible for black, but they all lead to checkmate, so...

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:06.700
It doesn't really matter what black plays.

00:05:07.669 --> 00:05:16.100
So yeah, this is the position and this is move number 7, where Bobby played bishop to b3.

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:26.800
This great grandmaster, Samuel Reshevsky, makes such a huge blunder on move 8 and plays knight to a5.

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:29.840
And, well, the rest is history.

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:34.720
So yeah, that's the game. I hope you enjoyed it and see you soon.

